# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: alfe-cad-load-error-message-says-only-error — a CAD-side failure is reported without naming its cause
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P2
#+TYPE: alfe-backend
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: -
#+DATE: 2026-09-26

* Observed

verify:epure-api:windows on master (alfe 2.2.96, job 16753534728), the
BricsCAD run that loads pjb's two expressions from a file. The failure
is now REPORTED (that is alfe-eval-x-output-lost-on-cad, fixed in
2.2.91) -- but what it says is:

#+begin_example
protocol: status now "DONE 1 FAIL" (elapsed 0.06 s)
ERROR protocol request 1: while loading C:\msys64\tmp\alfe-epure-api.lsp
  (form starting at line 1) at line 1, column 1: error
ERROR protocol request 1: while loading C:\msys64\tmp\alfe-epure-api.lsp
  (form starting at line 1) at line 1, column 1: error
cli: plan finished status=:FAILED exit=1
#+end_example

The cause is the single word =error=. The file's first form is
=(print (= "" (toutes_options nil)))=, and the message does not say
whether TOUTES_OPTIONS is undefined, was called with the wrong argument
count, or raised something itself -- which is exactly what the user
needs in order to act. "at line 1, column 1" is the position of the
whole form, so it does not narrow it down either.

(The duplicate line is a separate, already-filed defect:
alfe-cad-error-reported-twice-on-stderr.)

* Questions to settle

1. Where does the word =error= come from? Candidates: BricsCAD's own
   =*error*= message text for this condition; the runtime's
   =autolisp-effective-error-message= falling back to a placeholder when
   the message is empty; or =autolisp-source-load='s wrapper discarding
   the inner message while keeping its own frame.
2. Does a DIRECT =-x= of the same form give a better message? The eval
   path and the load path reach the error differently
   (=autolisp-eval-load-form= versus the alfe-eval.lsp load), and the
   comparison localises the loss.
3. Does the CAD have more to say elsewhere -- =protocol/stderr.txt= raw,
   the session log, =*AUTOLISP_ERROR_MSG*= -- that the report drops?

The clautolisp-hosted harness added in
alfe-autocad-shadow-path-spurious-newlines answers 1 and 2 without a
CAD: it can load a file whose form signals and show exactly what the
runtime captures. What it cannot answer is what BricsCAD itself puts in
the message; that needs the Windows runner.

* Acceptance

- A CAD-side failure names its cause: the engine's own message text, or
  an explicit statement that the engine supplied none.
- A regression test pins the message for a form that signals in the
  hosted harness.
